### How do you model commands?

Commands are immutable records that carry the intent to change state. They are plain DTOs with no behavior.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.command;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Positive;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record PlaceOrderCommand(
        @NotBlank String orderId,
        @NotBlank String accountId,
        @Positive BigDecimal amount
) {}

public record ShipOrderCommand(
        @NotBlank String orderId
) {}
```

```text
Command example:
PlaceOrderCommand(orderId=ORD-001, accountId=SAV2001, amount=25000.00)
```

### How do you build a rich aggregate that emits events?

The aggregate is a JPA entity that owns its state and exposes behavior. It extends `AbstractAggregateRoot` from Spring Data to register events that are published automatically on save.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.command;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import org.springframework.data.domain.AbstractAggregateRoot;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;

@Entity
@Table(name = "orders")
public class Order extends AbstractAggregateRoot<Order> {

    @Id
    private String id;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)
    private OrderStatus status;

    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private Instant createdAt;

    protected Order() {} // JPA

    public Order(String id, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.id = id;
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.amount = amount;
        this.status = OrderStatus.CREATED;
        this.createdAt = Instant.now();
    }

    public void place() {
        if (this.status != OrderStatus.CREATED) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot place order in status: " + status);
        }
        this.status = OrderStatus.PLACED;
        registerEvent(new OrderPlacedEvent(this.id, this.accountId, this.amount));
    }

    public void ship() {
        if (this.status != OrderStatus.PLACED) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Cannot ship order in status: " + status);
        }
        this.status = OrderStatus.SHIPPED;
        registerEvent(new OrderShippedEvent(this.id));
    }

    public String getId() { return id; }
    public OrderStatus getStatus() { return status; }
    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
    public BigDecimal getAmount() { return amount; }
}

public enum OrderStatus { CREATED, PLACED, SHIPPED, DELIVERED, CANCELLED }
```

```text
Order state machine:
  CREATED -> place() -> PLACED -> ship() -> SHIPPED -> deliver() -> DELIVERED
                                    |
                              cancel() -> CANCELLED
```

### How do domain events look?

Domain events are simple records describing what happened.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.command;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record OrderPlacedEvent(String orderId, String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {}
public record OrderShippedEvent(String orderId) {}
```

### How does the command handler work?

The command handler is a `@Service` that loads the aggregate, invokes its method, and saves. The `@Transactional` boundary ensures the change and event publication happen together.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.command;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class OrderCommandHandler {

    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;

    public OrderCommandHandler(OrderRepository orderRepo) {
        this.orderRepo = orderRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public String handle(PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order order = new Order(cmd.orderId(), cmd.accountId(), cmd.amount());
        order.place();
        orderRepo.save(order);
        System.out.println("Order placed: " + cmd.orderId());
        return cmd.orderId();
    }

    @Transactional
    public void handle(ShipOrderCommand cmd) {
        Order order = orderRepo.findById(cmd.orderId()).orElseThrow();
        order.ship();
        orderRepo.save(order);
        System.out.println("Order shipped: " + cmd.orderId());
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Order placed: ORD-001
Order shipped: ORD-001
```

### How does the projection listener update the read model?

The key choice is `@TransactionalEventListener(phase = AFTER_COMMIT)`, which means the projection runs only after the write transaction commits successfully.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.query;

import com.bank.cqrs.command.OrderPlacedEvent;
import com.bank.cqrs.command.OrderShippedEvent;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionalEventListener;
import org.springframework.transaction.event.TransactionPhase;

@Component
public class OrderSummaryProjection {

    private final OrderSummaryRepository readRepo;

    public OrderSummaryProjection(OrderSummaryRepository readRepo) {
        this.readRepo = readRepo;
    }

    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void onOrderPlaced(OrderPlacedEvent event) {
        OrderSummaryView view = new OrderSummaryView();
        view.setOrderId(event.orderId());
        view.setAccountId(event.accountId());
        view.setAmount(event.amount());
        view.setStatus("PLACED");
        readRepo.save(view);
        System.out.println("Read model created for order: " + event.orderId());
    }

    @TransactionalEventListener(phase = TransactionPhase.AFTER_COMMIT)
    public void onOrderShipped(OrderShippedEvent event) {
        OrderSummaryView view = readRepo.findById(event.orderId()).orElseThrow();
        view.setStatus("SHIPPED");
        readRepo.save(view);
        System.out.println("Read model updated to SHIPPED: " + event.orderId());
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Read model created for order: ORD-001
Read model updated to SHIPPED: ORD-001
```

### What does the read model entity look like?

A separate denormalized JPA entity with exactly the columns a query screen needs.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.query;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "order_summary_view")
public class OrderSummaryView {

    @Id
    private String orderId;
    private String accountId;
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private String status;

    // No business logic here, just data for queries
    // Getters and setters
    public String getOrderId() { return orderId; }
    public void setOrderId(String orderId) { this.orderId = orderId; }
    public String getAccountId() { return accountId; }
    public void setAccountId(String accountId) { this.accountId = accountId; }
    public BigDecimal getAmount() { return amount; }
    public void setAmount(BigDecimal amount) { this.amount = amount; }
    public String getStatus() { return status; }
    public void setStatus(String status) { this.status = status; }
}
```

### How do you separate controllers?

Two controllers: one for commands (POST/PUT) and one for queries (GET). This makes responsibilities obvious and allows independent scaling.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.api;

import com.bank.cqrs.command.*;
import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/orders")
public class OrderCommandController {

    private final OrderCommandHandler handler;

    public OrderCommandController(OrderCommandHandler handler) {
        this.handler = handler;
    }

    @PostMapping
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.CREATED)
    public String placeOrder(@RequestBody PlaceOrderCommand cmd) {
        return handler.handle(cmd);
    }

    @PostMapping("/{id}/ship")
    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.ACCEPTED)
    public void shipOrder(@PathVariable String id) {
        handler.handle(new ShipOrderCommand(id));
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.cqrs.api;

import com.bank.cqrs.query.*;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.util.List;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/orders")
public class OrderQueryController {

    private final OrderSummaryRepository readRepo;

    public OrderQueryController(OrderSummaryRepository readRepo) {
        this.readRepo = readRepo;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public OrderSummaryView getOrder(@PathVariable String id) {
        return readRepo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    }

    @GetMapping(params = "accountId")
    public List<OrderSummaryView> getByAccount(@RequestParam String accountId) {
        return readRepo.findByAccountId(accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
GET /api/v1/orders/ORD-001 returns:
{"orderId":"ORD-001","accountId":"SAV2001","amount":25000.00,"status":"SHIPPED"}
```

### Why is this maintainable without a heavyweight framework?

Every piece is a standard Spring concept: records, JPA entities, `@Service`, `@Transactional`, `@TransactionalEventListener`, and `JpaRepository`. There is no event store to operate, no snapshotting, no replay machinery.

```java
package com.bank.cqrs;

public class WhyNoFramework {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Building blocks used (all standard Spring):");
        System.out.println("  - Java records for commands");
        System.out.println("  - JPA entity extending AbstractAggregateRoot");
        System.out.println("  - @Service + @Transactional command handlers");
        System.out.println("  - @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) projections");
        System.out.println("  - JpaRepository for write and read repos");
        System.out.println("  - Separate REST controllers");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("No Axon, no event store, no replay engine.");
        System.out.println("Graduate to heavier machinery only when genuinely needed.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Building blocks used (all standard Spring):
  - Java records for commands
  - JPA entity extending AbstractAggregateRoot
  - @Service + @Transactional command handlers
  - @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) projections
  - JpaRepository for write and read repos
  - Separate REST controllers

No Axon, no event store, no replay engine.
Graduate to heavier machinery only when genuinely needed.
```

### What are the caveats?

The `AFTER_COMMIT` listener runs outside the original transaction, so the projection must be idempotent. If it must survive a crash, upgrade to the transactional outbox pattern.

```text
Caveats:
  1. AFTER_COMMIT runs in a NEW transaction -> projection must be idempotent
  2. If the app crashes after commit but before projection -> read model is stale
     -> Fix: use outbox pattern for crash-safe projection updates
  3. Projection rebuild: if schema changes, replay from write model or events
  4. This is NOT event sourcing: the write model is a normal JPA entity, not an event log
```
